WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.510
SPEAKER: Since leaping into
the world of serverless,

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.920
Critter Junction's been
reaching new limits.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.860
But I've got a few tricks to
take it up to the next level.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:08.850
Stay tuned.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.225
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.450
A core promise of serverless
compute platforms,

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.400
like Cloud Run and
Cloud Functions,

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.160
is that you don't need to worry
about your infrastructure.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.190
Your service scales
automatically.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.350
That works great when your
whole stack autoscales.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.500
But what if your
service depends on API

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.607
or databases with rate
or connection limits?

00:00:36.607 --> 00:00:38.440
That's what happened
with Critter Junction's

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.260
new companion app code
named the Wallet app.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.380
It allows users to send and
receive virtual currency

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.190
over a marketplace.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.520
Every time a user
sends or receives cash,

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.890
the change to the user
Wallet data in the database

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:55.240
kicks off a Cloud Function
to return a confirmation.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.600
Cloud Function is a lightweight,
serverless compute solution

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.510
that lets you create single
purpose and standalone

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.120
functions that respond
to cloud events.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.490
And because serverless compute
products are stateless,

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.870
they use Cloud SQL, a fully
managed relational database

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.530
service for stateful data,
like user Wallet amounts.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.920
After the Wallet app gained
popularity because of YouTube

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.010
live streamers, Cloud SQL's
maximum connection limit

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.150
was reached.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.130
And that meant the
upstream functions

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.540
weren't able to provide knew
Wallet amount confirmations.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.640
So my team came in to
give them a few tips

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.610
to scale the Wallet app
and any future serverless

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.460
workloads they plan to deploy.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.240
Let's review how
serverless functions

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.350
scale as you take a function
from your local computer

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.090
to the cloud.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.140
A function takes inputs and
provides an output response.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.450
That function can be
repeated with many inputs

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.790
and provide many outputs.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.900
A serverless platform manages
the elastic horizontal scaling

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.970
of function instances.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.270
Because Google Cloud can
give you near infinite scale,

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.550
that can have consequences
for other systems

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.080
that your function
interacts with.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.752
So what do you do?

00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:09.460
Well, a lot of what
I'm about to tell you

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.050
involves applying
limits to operations

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.020
to protect underlying
services and resources.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.805
First off, you can configure
instance scaling limits

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:20.320
to your functions.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.830
Cloud Functions offer
max instance settings.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.950
This feature limits how
many concurrent instances

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.800
of your functions are running
and attempting to establish

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.810
database connections.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.960
So if your database
can only handle

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.100
100 concurrent connections, you
might set your max instances

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.280
to a lower value, say 75.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.080
Since each instance
of a function

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.680
can only handle a single
request at a time,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.890
this means you can only
handle 75 concurrent requests

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:54.490
at a given time, saving your
database from maxing out

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.940
at its rate limit
even during a spike.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.300
Second, use Cloud Tasks to
limit the rate of work done.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.160
Sometimes, the limit
you're worried about

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.200
isn't the number of
concurrent connections,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.870
but the rate at which
work is performed.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.610
For example, imagine you need
a call to an external API that

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.400
has limited per minute quota.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.470
Cloud Tasks's a
fully managed service

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.110
that lets you manage
the execution, dispatch,

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.990
and delivery of
distributed tasks.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.730
You can manage the
way work gets done

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:28.240
and perform the work outside of
the serverless handlers in work

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.440
queues.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.520
Cloud Tasks supports rate
and concurrency limits.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.170
So regardless of its rate
at which work arrives,

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:38.150
it's performed
with rates applied.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.980
For example, if large influx
of API requests come in,

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.770
Cloud Tasks can buffer the
rate at which your request can

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:46.810
be handled by the API.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:49.390
Third, use stateful storage
to defer the results

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.303
from long-running operations.

00:03:51.303 --> 00:03:52.720
Sometimes, you
want your functions

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.020
to be able to defer work
that might take a while,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.790
but still give a
response to the caller

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.040
that the task was received.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.890
You want to send the results
of the task to the caller

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:08.330
eventually, also known as
long-running operations.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.120
For example, it
might not make sense

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.390
to encode a large video file
inside a serverless instance.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.200
You could use Cloud Tasks if
the caller of your workload

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.438
only needs to know that
their request was submitted.

00:04:20.438 --> 00:04:22.480
But if you want the caller
to be able to retrieve

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.850
a status or eventual
result, you need

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:28.057
an additional stateful
system to track that job.

00:04:28.057 --> 00:04:29.890
There are several ways
you can do this using

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:34.510
combination of Cloud Functions,
Cloud Pub/Sub, and Firestore.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.570
While Critter Junction has just
started to explore serverless

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.660
with cloud native apps, like
the layout and wallet apps,

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.660
they're constantly revisiting
how to improve performance

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.070
and scalability, like using
max serverless instances,

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.140
Cloud Tasks to limit
the rate of work,

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.740
and stateful storage for
long-running operations.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.840
For more details on these tips
and an awesome decision tree,

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.270
check out the link below.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:01.390
And stay tuned for our next
serverless scaling tips.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:04.450
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

